      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Let $a_{n}$ be an arithmetic progression with first term $a_{1}=2$ and common difference $d_{1}=3$. Then the generic term of the arithmetic progression is $2n+1$. \n\nLet $b_{n}$ be an arithmetic progression with first term $b_{1}=3$ and common difference $d_{2}=7$. Then the generic term of this arithmetic progression is $3n+6 = a_{n-3}$. \n\n$1$. Find the smallest number greater than $1$ that is common to both arithmetic progressions. \n\nFrom $a_{n} = 2n+1$ and $b_{n} = a_{n-3} = 2n+5$, we conclude that these sequence values are only their first terms. \nTherefore, the smallest number greater than $1$ that is common to both arithmetic progressions is $3.$\n\n$2$. Find the largest number less than $500$ that is common to both arithmetic progressions. \n\nWe know that $(2k+1)$ is greater than $1$ for all non-negative integers $k,$ and that $(2k-1)$ is less than $500$ for all integers $k \\leq 249,$ and that $249$ is the largest non-negative integer less than $250$.\nThere is at least one integer $k \\leq 250$ such that $2(k-1)+1 \\leq 500$.\n\nThus, the largest common value less than $500$ is $(2(k-1)+ 1) =  250*2+1 = 501.$",
